\begin_layout Title
Convert 3D Electric Field Polarization Vector To 2D Jones Vector
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can convert a 3D electric field polarization vector to a 2D Jones vector provided that the wave is a transverse plane wave (paraxial approximation) and you define a local 2D coordinate basis perpendicular to its propagation direction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a Jones vector only tracks the two transverse components of the electric field converting from 3D world space requires projecting the 3D vector onto the local plane perpendicular to the photon's wavevector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Mathematical Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose a photon is traveling along a unit propagation direction 
\begin_inset Formula ${\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\Vert{}\hat{\mathbf{k}}\Vert{}=1})$
\end_inset

 with a complex 3D electric field vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D}}=(E_{x},E_{y},E_{z})^{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Ensure Transversality:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 For a plane wave in free space,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D}}$
\end_inset

 must be perpendicular to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{\hat{k}}}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D}}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{k}}=0})$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

If your raytracer produces slight numerical drift strip any longitudinal component first:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}_{\perp}=\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D}}-(\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D}}\cdot\mathbf{k})\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Construct a Local 2D Orthonormal Basis 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\mathbf{s}},\hat{\mathbf{p}})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Choose two orthogonal unit vectors in the plane perpendicular to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 (such as the standard s- and p-polarization vectors relative to an optical axis or global up-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{u}})$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}=\frac{\mathbf{\hat{k}}\times\hat{\mathbf{u}}}{\|\mathbf{\mathbf{\hat{k}}}\times\hat{\mathbf{u}}\|},\qquad\mathbf{\hat{p}}=\mathbf{\hat{k}}\times\mathbf{\hat{s}}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

(Note:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{k}}$
\end_inset

 is parallel to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{u}}$
\end_inset

 then fall back to an alternate reference vector like 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{x}}$
\end_inset

 to avoid a zero cross product).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Project and Normalize:
 Dot the 3D field with your local basis vectors to get the 2D Jones vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
|\psi\rangle & = & \frac{1}{\|\mathbf{E}_{\perp}\|}\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{E}_{\perp}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{s}}\\
\mathbf{E}_{\perp}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{p}}
\end{matrix}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
 When This Breaks Down (Limitations)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Tightly Focused Beams / High Numerical Aperture (NA):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Near a tight focal spot (like Freedman's f/0.8 lenses),
 light develops a strong longitudinal electric field component (
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{E_{z}\neq0\$}$
\end_inset

) along 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{\hat{k}}})$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

A 2D Jones vector discards this longitudinal component;
 you must use a 3 × 3 polarization coherency matrix or full vector diffraction instead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Non-Planar / Off-Axis Ray Tracing:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 When a ray reflects or refracts in 3D space the local 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\mathbf{s}},\hat{\mathbf{p}})$
\end_inset

 basis rotates.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 In 3D ray tracers it is often cleaner to keep the polarization as a 3D complex vector using 3 × 3 polarization ray-tracing matrices (Yun,
 Crabtree,
 & Chipman) and only project to a 2D Jones vector right at the polarizer's surface plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note:
 After the photon passes through the polarizer and collapses to a 2D state 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\psi_{\text{out}}\rangle$
\end_inset

 you can reconstruct its 3D field vector via 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{E}_{3\text{D, out}}=E_{x}\hat{\mathbf{s}}+E_{y}\hat{\mathbf{p}}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are integrating this into a 3D optical geometry,
 let me know:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Whether your photons hit the polarizer at oblique angles of incidence (which shifts the effective transmission axis and Fresnel reflection)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you also need the inverse function (To_3D_Vector) to propagate the collapsed photon onward in 3D space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Whether you are modeling collection cone angles (like Freedman's f/0.8 lenses where rays fan out across multiple 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{\hat{k}}}$
\end_inset

 directions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I can provide the exact geometric transformations for your setup.
\end_layout

